{"question":{"id":"exponential_maximum_gumbel_known_result","title":"Gumbel limit for the maximum of exponential observations","description":"Focused L0 known-result evaluation grounded in Fisher and Tippett (1928) and Gnedenko (1943). Let X_1,...,X_n be independent rate-one exponential random variables, let M_n=max_i X_i, and let Y_n=M_n-log n. In one authoritative Markdown/LaTeX Theory workspace, derive the exact finite-n CDF of M_n and the support-aware finite-n CDF of Y_n, including the branch below -log n. Then prove pointwise for every real y that P(Y_n <= y) converges to the standard Gumbel CDF exp(-exp(-y)). Make independence, the exponential support, the normalization log n, the fixed-y limiting argument, and the distinction between an exact finite-n law and its asymptotic approximation auditable. Do not replace the maximum by the minimum, omit the finite-n support branch, center by n or log(n-1), reverse a CDF or survival function, claim finite-n equality with the Gumbel law, or generalize the result to arbitrary parent distributions without domain-of-attraction conditions. The required model-authored Python artifact is est_exponential_maximum_gumbel and must implement run_estimator(request) exactly for the frozen closed sample contract. It returns the sample size, maximum, centered maximum, exact finite-n CDF evaluated at that centered value, and standard Gumbel CDF evaluated at that value. Exploratory Python or R may check identities. A separately frozen confirmatory assessment generates independent rate-one exponential samples at several predeclared sample sizes, invokes the exact accepted estimator, verifies the exact finite-n CDF empirically with Monte Carlo uncertainty, and assesses the Gumbel approximation over a preregistered finite grid as n grows. This assessment is distributional implementation evidence, not proof, a universal extreme-value theorem, finite-n Gumbel equality, source replication, novelty, tail extrapolation beyond the frozen grid, or formal proof. Lean formalization is not applicable.","tags":["extreme value theory","exponential distribution","sample maximum","Gumbel distribution","weak convergence","known result","L0"]},"workspace_context":{"theory_packet_id":"theory_derivation:aba8e12e19d3c2002437c71b","theory_context":{"source_theory_packet_id":"theory_derivation:aba8e12e19d3c2002437c71b","document_authoritative":true,"theory_content_authority":"model_authored_markdown_latex_documents","structured_handoff_role":"structured_cross_agent_index_and_abi","theory_derivation_trace":{"claim_index":[{"id":"thm.exact_cdf_M_n","kind":"theorem","document_path":"exponential_maximum_gumbel_theory.md","status":"SUPPORTED"},{"id":"thm.exact_cdf_Y_n","kind":"theorem","document_path":"exponential_maximum_gumbel_theory.md","depends_on":["thm.exact_cdf_M_n"],"status":"SUPPORTED"},{"id":"def.gumbel_cdf","kind":"definition","document_path":"exponential_maximum_gumbel_theory.md","status":"SUPPORTED"},{"id":"thm.pointwise_convergence","kind":"theorem","document_path":"exponential_maximum_gumbel_theory.md","depends_on":["thm.exact_cdf_Y_n","def.gumbel_cdf"],"status":"SUPPORTED"},{"id":"eq.support_constraint","kind":"equation","document_path":"exponential_maximum_gumbel_theory.md","status":"SUPPORTED"},{"id":"eq.approximation_error","kind":"equation","document_path":"exponential_maximum_gumbel_theory.md","depends_on":["thm.exact_cdf_Y_n","def.gumbel_cdf"],"status":"SUPPORTED"},{"id":"est_exponential_maximum_gumbel","kind":"definition","document_path":"est_exponential_maximum_gumbel.md","depends_on":["thm.exact_cdf_M_n","thm.exact_cdf_Y_n","def.gumbel_cdf"],"status":"SUPPORTED"}],"sanity_check_index":[{"id":"check.example_n10_y0","claim_ref":"thm.exact_cdf_Y_n","document_path":"exponential_maximum_gumbel_theory.md","status":"PASS"},{"id":"check.support_boundary","claim_ref":"eq.support_constraint","document_path":"exponential_maximum_gumbel_theory.md","status":"PASS"},{"id":"check.gumbel_limit_y0","claim_ref":"thm.